 [{:cue "I think" :nodes ["R1" "R3" "R14"] :why "Epistemic modality can state a standing belief, introduce a revised belief, or soften a commitment. The System 4 object split does not separate commitment temperature, and within System 4 the cue alone does not distinguish having from doing."}
  {:cue "I don't know" :nodes ["R1" "R3a" "R6"] :why "It can report an uncertain belief, acknowledge that an account failed, or leave the action space open. Neither the Max-Neef nor object split resolves the cue without its complement."}
  {:cue "I see" :nodes ["R2" "R8"] :why "Direct observation can be neutral evidence or evidence of present-fit mismatch. The understanding having/being split requires mismatch language beyond the evidential marker."}
  {:cue "it still says" :nodes ["R2" "R8"] :why "It marks a world observation and often also a discrepancy against an expected state. The understanding having/being split is not separable from this phrase alone."}
  {:cue "test" :nodes ["R2" "R9" "R12"] :why "A test may produce an observation, provide independent audit, or calibrate a score against reality. The Beer split between System 4 and System 3* and the latter's Max-Neef split need context about purpose and independence."}
  {:cue "verify" :nodes ["R9" "R12" "GRAIN-GATE"] :why "Verification may enforce independent checking, assess calibration, or enforce admissibility. System 3* versus System 3 is not identified by the verb alone."}
  {:cue "in order" :nodes ["R13" "R15"] :why "It may specify a multi-step policy or a dependency hierarchy across levels. The Beer System 4/System 2 split needs evidence about planning versus coordination."}
  {:cue "next" :nodes ["R6" "R13" "R14"] :why "It can ask which action is available, identify a planned future step, or commit to one action. The System 3/System 4 split and System 3 Max-Neef split both need more context."}
  {:cue "go ahead" :nodes ["R16" "R14"] :why "The phrase both commits to an action and authorizes its enactment. The Beer System 3/System 1 split fails unless selection and execution are allowed to co-occur."}
  {:cue "run" :nodes ["R10" "R16" "R2"] :why "It may concern liveness, command an execution, or report an observation produced by execution. The first Beer split cannot classify the bare verb."}
  {:cue "work" :nodes ["R16" "R10" "R9" "R17" "R19"] :why "Max-Neef places work in several cells, including creation-doing, subsistence-having, participation-having, identity-having, and creation-having. It is not discriminative at either the Beer or need split."}
  {:cue "what I want" :nodes ["R19" "R1"] :why "It may express preference or merely describe the operator model. System 5 versus System 4 depends on whether the utterance sets policy or reports a belief about policy."}
  {:cue "stop the line" :nodes ["R20" "CTAU-CLASS" "GRAIN-GATE"] :why "It can be an alarm, a class-level preference allocation, or an admissibility rule. The Beer split fails without knowing whether the turn signals affect, specifies utility, or constrains support."}
  {:cue "cheap enough" :nodes ["R11" "R5"] :why "It can describe a shared resource ceiling or an expected-cost trade-off. System 2 versus the System 3–4 homeostat requires the object being costed."}
  {:cue "rework" :nodes ["R16" "R17"] :why "It can request ordinary implementation or a change to the model's categories and relations. Creation-doing appears under both System 1 and System 4, so the Max-Neef split cannot separate them."}
  {:cue "phase" :nodes ["R15" "CTAU-CLASS" "R13"] :why "A phase can denote nested timescale, changing whole-system priorities, or a step in a policy. The Beer split needs the role the phase plays."}]
